    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8bc09474_54b36c83",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 16,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-23T22:49:01Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But `rddkeepalive` is not a prerequisite of running FAFT and lab deployment, yet.\n\nI think `rddkeepalive` is more like a trick to debug CCD issue. If we want to make `rddkeepalive` official, need to evaluate any test coverage is sacrificed and any side-effect, like power measurement, false alarm bugs on CCD/servo detection, etc. I don\u0027t object to this path, but need wider audiences to comment (need a bug to track).\n\nThe crrev.com/c/277275 actually still serves its original purpose. The CCD connection keeps connected, without needing any trick. I said missing VBUS is not perfect. Why don\u0027t we fix the VBUS? Some approaches are:\n* on DUT side, use CONFIG_POWER_PP5000_CONTROL and add the PD task dependency, so PP5000 is still on in S5;\n* or on servo v4 side, make servo v4 act like 1st-generation Susy-Q, which is powered by external VBUS, so servo v4 won\u0027t complain.",
      "parentUuid": "a6bc5c41_859def55",
      "revId": "0fc1c277b2a459a4500b8315d50e015157e76162",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f7ecb4f1_787f08e4",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 16,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125984
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-24T03:07:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If it wasn\u0027t explicit, it should be. We already establish it as a the default when servod is launched which would be the case for FAFT and the lab. I\u0027m not sure what else besides documentation would make it more explicit. The reason it was introduced in the first place was because normal Type-C behaviour including DRP toggling as well as USB PD traffic caused the CC line voltages to be out of range for the Rd detector block in H1. This would cause false disconnections. See b/35586249 for more background. It\u0027s not servo v4 complaining, it\u0027s the voltage going out of range on H1. The smaug implementation preceded H1 so I assume that it works differently.\n\nThe problem with fixing VBUS is that not every platform can source VBUS in S5 even if we wanted to. Some devices use the signal that generally indicates the ACPI S5 power state as the enable to the enable pin on the voltage regulator that generates VBUS \u0026 VCONN.",
      "parentUuid": "8bc09474_54b36c83",
      "revId": "0fc1c277b2a459a4500b8315d50e015157e76162",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "068cb8dd_19fbea6b",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 16,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000407
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-11-25T15:59:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I didn\u0027t aware that servod init calls `rddkeepalive`. This may explain some mysterious behaviors when I saw, like measuring power, testing DP alt-mode (the CCD_MODE_ODL mux blocking the SBU lines).\n\nAgree that the `rddkeepalive` is currently a decent workaround for this kind of CC out of voltage issues. When moving to Servo v4.1, the DAC is a better workaround for this issue. It doesn\u0027t leave DUT in a CCD always-connected state, even CCD is not used. We may deprecate the `rddkeepalive` that time.\n\nSince the `rddkeepalive` is already in place, I don\u0027t object to pushing this CL, which helps aligning the CrOS power sourcing policy between debug accessories and normal devices, as long as Edward does the tests in case anything is missing.",
      "parentUuid": "f7ecb4f1_787f08e4",
      "revId": "0fc1c277b2a459a4500b8315d50e015157e76162",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
